Context: pp. 650bis–787 of the continuation of 'La Longue Marche' (c. 1981). In these pages Grothendieck builds group schemes and pro-group schemes over Z, or over a base S, out of discrete groups, presumably aiming at arithmetic and schematic models of fundamental and Teichmüller-type groups. The setting, as far as it can be reconstructed: S = Spec A is a scheme with S_0 (probably Spec Z) below it, and U = S[1/n] with ring A_n = A[1/n]. G = Spec B is affine of finite type over S, and E^1_U is the affine line, i.e. the additive group G_a, over U.

Previous page. A morphism u: G_{a,U} -> G_U corresponds to psi: B_n -> A_n[T]. Precomposing u with multiplication by m (written phi_m, T -> mT, formula (24)) sends sum a_i T^i to sum m^i a_i T^i. If m = n^nu with nu large, every coefficient m^i a_i with i >= 1 lies in A. Hence psi_m(x) lies in A[T] if and only if a_0 lies in A, where a_0 = x evaluated at T = 0 (formula (25)).

This page.
- Grothendieck applies (25) to a finite set of algebra generators of B. Corollary: for G affine of finite type over S (smooth, 'to fix ideas about group structures') and u: E^1_U -> G_U a morphism of schemes, there is a power m = n^nu such that u composed with phi_m extends to S if and only if u(0) extends to a section of G over S.
- In particular, if u is a group homomorphism, then u(0) = e extends automatically. So some n^nu-rescaling of u extends over S. By schematic density (previous page) the extension is again a homomorphism of group schemes.
- Remark: the argument proves the 'théorème' referred to on p. 662' when S is noetherian of dimension 1. The case of interest is a Dedekind-type base whose generic point has characteristic 0, e.g. Spec Z.

New section (margin title: 'Schémas en groupes associés aux ext. des groupes discrets'). Let M be a free Z-module of finite type. Grothendieck announces a pro-group scheme over S attached to the projective system of finite-index subgroups of M. The next page sets up the general framework:
- an extension 1 -> M -> 𝔊 -> H -> 1 of discrete groups with M commutative;
- the vector group M_ (A -> M ⊗ A);
- the constant group H_{S_0};
- an exact sequence of Groups 1 -> M_ -> 𝔊_ -> H_ -> 1 over S_0, whose Z-points recover the original extension;
- the construction 𝔊_ = 𝔊_{S_0} ×^{M_{S_0}} M_, i.e. pushing out the constant extension along M -> M_.

The rescaling lemma is what lets one show that homomorphisms from G_a defined away from n become integral after replacing the generator by an n^nu-multiple. This is needed to compare the 'linearised' group M ⊗ A with the system of finite-index sublattices n^nu M.
